{"kind":"assert","text":"Review for Hugh: name the browser acceptance surface before work starts.\n\nReader renewal #20545 asked for an owned \"native browser\" tab. The partial account #20635 treated that as Google Chrome: Chrome automation could not select a usable window, while owned in-app-browser checks had exercised Table/Graph, standalone Notes, source and attachment previews. The Chrome-specific evidence remained unobserved, but the wording made it unclear whether that specific browser was essential to the product outcome. I introduced the ambiguous wording in the request; this is a commissioning problem to fix.\n\nThe [attached evidence](browser-acceptance-surface-evidence.json) preserves both records and the prospective correction in rollout #20880. That child explicitly accepts an owned available browser, including the in-app browser, for the actual served localhost application and the full interaction journey. It still requires real rendering, correct source/build identity, highlighted larger-file windows, history/reload and evidence navigation. It expressly leaves the old Chrome-specific acceptance unperformed. The rollout is claimed, so this is a concrete wording example, not a claim of measured speedup or completed browser acceptance.\n\nRecommendation: use two short statements in future operational requests. First name the behavior and accepted surfaces: for example, \"Verify the served application in an owned controlled browser; Chrome or the in-app browser is acceptable. Record which one, the build, URLs and interactions.\" Then name any browser-specific condition separately: for example, \"This reported defect must also be reproduced and checked in Chrome.\" If a user names a browser or an issue depends on its behavior, that requirement stays binding.\n\nKeep three evidence facts distinct in the report: the reader's verified identity/build/readiness; the actual application journey performed; and any browser or automation surface that could not be exercised. An unavailable window-control provider is not proof that the application failed. An API response is not a browser test, and an in-app-browser result does not prove a Chrome-specific result.\n\nThis needs no new workflow state or approval gate. It makes acceptance concrete earlier and avoids discovering an implicit environment requirement after deployment. Apply it prospectively; do not rewrite old records, silently waive a named condition or declare #20545 complete from this note. Planner will explicitly resolve that older request's remaining disposition after the current-source rollout evidence is reviewed.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:13df401d08fbef773ba87b2a86e52c7fe040692c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:67faa2b1b56e51259b2945cdeade2f15744d332f stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:03270b167c8f14b55a31919b2489715ee513b20f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0438e5f5a6b2167feceb5a0c8646280a4227794c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:07e8e48166ea90a1d864c829f36902817926f6e7 and 99 more"}}